<section class="block">
<h2>What is reported</h2>
<p>Reports on 28 October 2026 say India is considering a new AI law that could create a separate safety framework for the most powerful AI models, requiring developers to show adequate safeguards before public release<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Options reportedly under discussion include pre-release evaluation, testing in controlled environments and sandbox requirements, with a consultation paper expected soon<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Earlier in October, the IT Minister was reported as saying a regulation blueprint would follow within a month<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, and coverage has also described proposals for safeguards on high-risk AI systems<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. None of this is final.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Who it could affect</h2>
<p>Frontier rules target the companies that build the most capable models. Most organisations in India build applications on top of models: they choose a model, add tools and data, and ship an agent or feature. That layering matters, because obligations tend to follow the layer you control.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/india-frontier-ai-law-what-it-means/agent-layers.webp" alt="Diagram of an AI agent framework above several AI agent applications, each built from a model and tools" width="564" height="231" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Applications combine a model with tools; frontier rules aim at the model layer, while deployer duties attach to the application. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2025/ai-agent-observability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Guangya Liu and Sujay Solomon</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Frontier developers</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Pre-release testing<em>reported focus</em></span><span>Sandbox requirements<em>under discussion</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Application builders</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Model choice and evidence<em>what you rely on</em></span><span class="hl">Deepfake and fraud controls<em>expected topics</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>All data fiduciaries</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">DPDP Rules<em>firm timelines already</em></span><span>Consent and retention<em>for AI uses too</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The proposed law is uncertain; DPDP obligations are not. Plan for both layers.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What to prepare now</h2>
<p>Whatever the final shape, the evidence regulators ask for tends to be the same: what AI you run, how you tested it, how you watch it in production, and how people can challenge it. Building that evidence is useful whether or not the law passes.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Inventory</b><span>Every AI system, the model behind it and its owner</span></li>
<li><b>Classify</b><span>Which uses could be high-risk: credit, hiring, health, identity</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Test</b><span>Evaluation sets and red-teaming before each release</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Monitor</b><span>Production tracing, incidents and drift</span></li>
<li><b>Label</b><span>Disclose AI-generated content and guard against impersonation</span></li>
<li><b>Engage</b><span>Read the consultation and respond with your evidence</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Evidence you build for safety is the evidence any regulator will ask for.</figcaption>
</figure>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Status</th><th>Action now</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Frontier model safety framework</td><td>Under consideration<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td><td>Track the consultation; check your model providers' testing evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deepfakes, fraud and impersonation</td><td>Expected in consultation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></td><td>Content labelling, identity checks and abuse monitoring</td></tr>
<tr><td>DPDP Rules</td><td>Notified November 2025, phasing in over 18 months<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup></td><td>Consent, retention and breach handling, including for AI uses</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><p>Do not wait for the final text to build an AI inventory and evaluation evidence. Those are the first things any version of the law will ask for.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>For most companies in India this is a watch-and-prepare item, not a fire drill. The firm deadline is the DPDP Rules, so put effort there first, and make sure AI uses are inside that programme rather than beside it. In parallel, build the inventory, evaluation and monitoring that any AI regime will expect, and ask your model providers what testing evidence they can share. Global teams will recognise the pattern from the <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-digital-omnibus-roadmap/">EU AI Act</a> and <a href="/case-studies/korea-ai-basic-act-hiring-platform/">Korea's AI Basic Act</a>. Related: <a href="/case-studies/india-dpdp-consent-platform-lending/">a DPDP-ready consent platform</a>, <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets that predict production</a> and <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-10-29/">tech news for 29 October</a>.</p>
</section>

